<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={schema} />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 text-slate-100">
    <header class="mb-10">
      <div class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-wider text-cyan-400 uppercase mb-3">Quantitative Derivatives • Volatility Modeling</div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-white mb-6 leading-tight">
        Vanna-Volga Pricing Method: Calculating Implied Volatility Smile in Python (2026)
      </h1>
      <p class="text-slate-400 text-lg leading-relaxed">
        A rigorous mathematical derivation of the Vanna-Volga approximation for FX and crypto options. Deriving second-order Greeks, interpolating the 25-Delta volatility smile, and implementing complete Python calibration code.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Google Quick Answer Callout -->
    <div class="bg-slate-900/80 border-l-4 border-cyan-500 rounded-xl p-6 mb-10 shadow-lg backdrop-blur-sm">
      <div class="text-xs uppercase font-bold text-cyan-400 mb-2">Google Quick Answer</div>
      <p class="text-base text-slate-200 leading-relaxed font-medium">
        <strong>The Vanna-Volga method is the standard quantitative framework for pricing vanilla and first-generation exotic options in FX and crypto markets.</strong> By decomposing an option's volatility risk into a portfolio of three liquid instruments (ATM, 25-Delta Call, and 25-Delta Put), the model hedges <strong>Vega, Vanna ($\frac{\partial^2 V}{\partial S \partial \sigma}$), and Volga ($\frac{\partial^2 V}{\partial \sigma^2}$)</strong>, delivering closed-form implied volatility smile calibration without expensive Monte Carlo simulations.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="prose prose-invert max-w-none space-y-8">
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">1. The Breakdown of Black-Scholes: Why Constant Volatility Fails</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          The classical Black-Scholes-Merton (BSM) differential equation assumes that the underlying asset price follows a geometric Brownian motion with constant volatility ($\sigma$). In real financial markets—particularly Foreign Exchange (EUR/USD, USD/JPY) and high-beta cryptocurrencies (BTC, ETH)—empirical option prices exhibit a prominent **volatility smile and skew**.
        </p>
        <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Out-of-the-money (OTM) puts trade at substantially higher implied volatilities than At-The-Money (ATM) options due to fat-tailed crash risk (skew), while deep OTM calls also command premiums due to speculative upside convexity (kurtosis). Because market makers quote options not in currency prices but in **Garman-Kohlhagen implied volatilities**, quants require a deterministic mapping function to interpolate volatilities across non-quoted strike prices ($K$).
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">2. The Three Anchor Liquid Quotes: ATM, Risk Reversal, and Butterfly</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          In professional FX and crypto derivatives desks, liquidity is concentrated in three standard market quotes for any given expiration ($T$):
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-300">
          <li><strong>At-The-Money Volatility ($\sigma_{ATM}$):</strong> The volatility of a delta-neutral straddle ($K_{ATM}$).</li>
          <li><strong>25-Delta Risk Reversal ($RR_{25}$):</strong> The volatility spread between the 25-delta call and 25-delta put:
            $$RR_{25} = \sigma(25\Delta C) - \sigma(25\Delta P)$$
            Measures the asymmetric market skew and directional crash fear.
          </li>
          <li><strong>25-Delta Butterfly ($BF_{25}$):</strong> The difference between the average of the 25-delta options and the ATM volatility:
            $$BF_{25} = \frac{\sigma(25\Delta C) + \sigma(25\Delta P)}{2} - \sigma_{ATM}$$
            Measures the market curvature (smile convexity / fat tails).
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">3. Mathematical Derivation of Vanna-Volga Weighting Coefficients</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          To price an target option with strike $K$, the Vanna-Volga method constructs a replicating portfolio consisting of the Black-Scholes price ($V_{BS}(K)$) plus a linear combination of three benchmark options ($K_1 = 25\Delta P$, $K_2 = ATM$, $K_3 = 25\Delta C$):
        </p>

        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 p-5 rounded-xl my-4 text-center">
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 mb-2">Vanna-Volga Option Price Formula:</p>
          <code class="text-base font-mono text-cyan-400">V_{VV}(K) = V_{BS}(K) + w_1(K) [V(K_1) - V_{BS}(K_1)] + w_2(K) [V(K_2) - V_{BS}(K_2)] + w_3(K) [V(K_3) - V_{BS}(K_3)]</code>
        </div>

        <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          By matching Vega ($\mathcal{V}$), Vanna ($\mathcal{V}_{anna}$), and Volga ($\mathcal{V}_{olga}$) across the portfolio, we solve the $3 \times 3$ linear system to obtain the closed-form Castagna-Mercurio weighting coefficients:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-slate-300 font-mono text-xs">
          <li>w_1(K) = [ln(K/K_2) * ln(K/K_3)] / [ln(K_1/K_2) * ln(K_1/K_3)]</li>
          <li>w_2(K) = [ln(K/K_1) * ln(K/K_3)] / [ln(K_2/K_1) * ln(K_2/K_3)]</li>
          <li>w_3(K) = [ln(K/K_1) * ln(K/K_2)] / [ln(K_3/K_1) * ln(K_3/K_2)]</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">4. Empirical Implied Volatility Smile Calibration (Python Code)</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          The following clean Python script implements the full Vanna-Volga smile interpolation, generating accurate implied volatilities across a continuous spectrum of strikes:
        </p>

        <div class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-800 text-xs font-mono overflow-x-auto my-4">
          <pre is:raw><code>import numpy as np
from scipy.stats import norm

def vanna_volga_smile(spot, r, q, T, sigma_atm, rr_25, bf_25, strikes):
    """
    Computes Vanna-Volga implied volatility smile for arbitrary strikes.
    """
    # 1. Recover the 3 liquid market volatilities
    sigma_call_25 = sigma_atm + bf_25 + 0.5 * rr_25
    sigma_put_25  = sigma_atm + bf_25 - 0.5 * rr_25
    
    # 2. Compute Forward Price
    F = spot * np.exp((r - q) * T)
    
    # 3. Compute 25-Delta and ATM Strikes
    k2 = F * np.exp(0.5 * (sigma_atm**2) * T) # K_ATM
    k1 = F * np.exp(norm.ppf(0.25) * sigma_put_25 * np.sqrt(T) + 0.5 * (sigma_put_25**2) * T) # K_Put_25
    k3 = F * np.exp(norm.ppf(0.75) * sigma_call_25 * np.sqrt(T) + 0.5 * (sigma_call_25**2) * T) # K_Call_25
    
    sigmas = []
    for K in strikes:
        # Compute Vanna-Volga weights
        w1 = (np.log(K / k2) * np.log(K / k3)) / (np.log(k1 / k2) * np.log(k1 / k3))
        w2 = (np.log(K / k1) * np.log(K / k3)) / (np.log(k2 / k1) * np.log(k2 / k3))
        w3 = (np.log(K / k1) * np.log(K / k2)) / (np.log(k3 / k1) * np.log(k3 / k2))
        
        # Second-order parabolic implied vol expansion
        sigma_K = w1 * sigma_put_25 + w2 * sigma_atm + w3 * sigma_call_25
        sigmas.append(sigma_K)
        
    return np.array(sigmas)

# Example Market Quote: BTC/USD 30-Day Options
spot = 65000.0
r = 0.045
q = 0.0
T = 30 / 365.0
sigma_atm = 0.52 # 52% ATM Vol
rr_25 = 0.04    # 4% Call Skew
bf_25 = 0.025   # 2.5% Butterfly Smile

test_strikes = np.linspace(50000, 80000, 7)
vols = vanna_volga_smile(spot, r, q, T, sigma_atm, rr_25, bf_25, test_strikes)

for k, v in zip(test_strikes, vols):
    print(f"Strike: ${k:,.0f} | Implied Vol: {v*100:.2f}%")</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">5. Comparison: Vanna-Volga vs Stochastic Volatility (SVI / Heston)</h2>

        <!-- Comparison Table -->
        <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
